friends this is an important day  this tenth anniversary of the September  11th 2001 terrorist attacks that day  reminded us that every day is an  important day to cling to those we love  to try to live as best we can so this  feels like an important moment to stand  before you and say wise things there is  nothing I can say though to make sense  of it to rewind the clock to restore a  sense of security individually or  nationally to bring peace or to take  away tragic memories or grief there are  gatherings later this afternoon to which  you are invited a community-wide  interfaith service on the green a  candlelight vigil tonight on cross  campus but our task this day in this  place is to worship God and to try to  listen for God's voice may God give us  what we need for the living of these  days in today's gospel text Peter asks  Jesus how often should I forgive I would  like to ask Jesus a few additional  questions  who should we forgive how do we forgive  and why should we forgive - Peters  question how often should I forgive  Jesus answers not seven times but I tell  you seventy-seven times some believe  that a more accurate translation is even  seventy times seven no matter how you do  the math the number seven symbolizes  completeness in short Jesus is saying  always always forgive again I want to  push Jesus who should we forgive always  how why a parable follows a slave owes  the king of ridiculous amount of money  ten thousand talents Matthew's Gospel  tells us just one talent represents many  years of wages for  laborer so in this story the laborer  owes his master many lifetime's worth of  wages this is just ridiculous  there is no way one person could owe  this much there is no king that would  hold that kind of debt over his laborer  the exaggerated numbers are part of the  point of this story uh tur dependence on  God cannot be distilled down into a  small tidy sum that we can repay but  let's follow the parable for a moment  the master tells his servant it's time  to pay up  ten thousand talents or he and his  family will be sold along with their  possessions of course he can't pay it's  absolutely impossible so he throws  himself down on his knees and begs he  pleads for mercy and miracle of miracles  the King forgives his debt all of it he  is free no longer owing his master such  an impossible sum there is no way to  quantify the size of debt we may owe to  our God the God who created us all the  God who hears our prayers even when  we're angry the God who hears the  hurtful things we say to one another and  feels the wounds of the hatred we  harbour the God whose heart breaks when  this world crumbles the God who knows us  completely and forgives us how can we  repay all of that when we take a good  look at our lives we see that each new  day is made possible by God's mercy  grace and forgiveness we are that  forgiving laborer set free by a God who  does not keep tally of our sin but  forgives us an infinite number of times  if only the parable ended there the  servant has just been forgiven his  impossibly huge debt  he's probably dancing down that street  he is free  and he bumps into one of his fellow  slaves who happens to owe him money  about a hundred days worth of wages and  so the forgiving man grabs that guy by  the throat and says pay what you owe the  second man for mercy he can't pay but  the forgiving man throws the other guy  in prison unbelievable how could someone  who is just experienced unbelievably  generous mercy not extended least some  of that forgiveness to his neighbor here  Jesus asks us to look closely in the  mirror how quickly we forget the  graciousness of God how quickly we start  pointing fingers and plotting revenge in  this parable we are both bound and free  both completely in debt to our God but  also completely forgiven with God  forgiveness has the last word so how  then shall we live leaders of the New  Haven and Yale communities chose three  words to commemorate today remember  renew and reflect these words call us to  pause to recall memories and share  stories to contemplate not only  September 11th 2001 but all that has  unfolded in the past decade even for  those of you who were quite young in  2001 each one of us has the task of  reflecting on that day in deciding how  we will tell the story to future  generations the final theme of this day  renew calls us to some sort of action  remember reflect renew when it comes to  Christ's call to forgive one another  these three words might offer a path to  forgiveness remember remember a time  when you were forgiven by someone else  it's hard to ask for forgiveness  but when forgiveness comes it is like a  fresh breeze blowing into the dusty  corners of our lives and it is a brand  new day all over again think back to an  experience of being forgiven by someone  else a sibling apparent friend have you  had to forgive yourself sometimes  remember what it feels like to be  forgiven reflect God's mercy is as wide  as the sea as deep as the deepest ocean  what does that mean reflect on that what  does it mean that we are a forgiving  people what does it mean that no matter  what we've done God loves us reflect on  a relationship that is strained in your  life reflect on why you find it hard to  forgive as people forgiven by God how  are we reflecting God's mercy to others  and renew is there a relationship in  your life in need of some extra  attention perhaps even some forgiveness  if there's some injustice in the world  that you think God is calling you to  speak out against to free others who  need to experience mercy how can you  make room for forgiveness in your life  this week every day but especially today  we must not put ourselves in the place  of God we do not decide the sentence  against those who have wronged us or  anyone we love instead today is a chance  to look collectively into the mirror of  the gospel to remember experiences of  mercy and grace to remember how  communities pulled together 10 years ago  and the many struggles people all over  the world have endured since how might  we extend God's mercy and forgiveness to  one another on this particularly tender  day  as Christ calls us to forgive over and  over again as we say that God's mercy is  as wide as the sea it is a it is  important to remember that forgiveness  has its limitations even the wide sea  has its boundaries forgiveness is  limited because it does not change the  past it does not justify bad behavior or  oppression forgiveness does not mean  forgetting preaching endless forgiveness  is dangerous many women and men too have  told their pastors about their spouse  abusing them and clergy have too often  turned to this very Gospel passage and  told them to forgive and forgive and  forgive and to stay in that abusive  relationship there are real limits to  forgiveness and safety physical and  emotional come first the call to forgive  does not mean we can ignore injustice  this is the church's ongoing task to  discern how to extend God's mercy mercy  and justice so that all may be freed  with all of its limitations forgiveness  still offers real benefits and this is  why the gospel calls us to forgive more  times than we can count  rabbi harold kushner tells the story of  a woman in his congregation who had come  to seek his counsel he described her as  a single mother divorced and working  really really hard to make ends meet and  support their three children she said  since my husband walked out on me every  month is a struggle to pay our bills I  have to tell my kids there is no money  for anything extra when he's living it  up in a new state with his new wife how  can you tell me to forgive him and the  rabbi answered her I'm not asking you to  forgive him because what he did was  acceptable it wasn't it was mean and  selfish I'm asking you to forgive  because he doesn't deserve the power to  in your head and turn you into a bitter  angry woman but you keep holding on to  him you're not hurting him by holding on  to years of resentment you might be  hurting yourself forgiveness can set us  free from the grip of bitterness and  anger and unforgiveness holding on to  past hurts can really have negative  effects on us as individuals and for the  thriving of a community author and the  Mott has written some very poignant  things about forgiveness and here are  three little snippets of her wisdom  forgiveness is giving up all hope of  having had a better past forgiveness  means it is finally unimportant that you  hit back not forgiving is like drinking  rat poison and then waiting for the rat  to die and she knows and the Mott knows  for a long time she harbored deep  resentment for those who hurt her she  had to prevent practice forgiving over  and over and over and then she had to  learn how to forgive herself over and  over and over but the hard work of  forgiving is worth it forgiveness is  more life-giving than wanting to rewrite  history  forgiveness is more life-giving than  wanting to always hit back  forgiveness is more life-giving than  drinking rat poison and waiting for that  rat to die and yet forgiveness is still  complicated it is not the answer to  everything I am just thankful that God  is so much better at forgiveness than we  are because God forgives us over and  over and over again we can summon this  drink to try to do the same it just may  be that the next decade of our life  together will depend on our ability to  listen to one  another to ask for mercy and to practice  forgiveness poet J janda a fist shaken  against anyone or anything can be  relaxed and the rock released to make  fire to cup water to bless the day
 

